00:14:57.096 --> 00:14:58.364
as concrete?

00:15:01.133 --> 00:15:02.534
It's just liquid stone.

00:15:02.534 --> 00:15:04.770
Didn't you just grind some
stones down or something

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:07.706
and then pour it out?

00:15:07.706 --> 00:15:10.910
Actually, if you think about
concrete and other materials

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:17.583
like steel, the use of
them is skyrocketing.

00:15:17.583 --> 00:15:20.886
This is how much we're making.

00:15:20.886 --> 00:15:24.657
So look at this, the
millions of tons.

00:15:24.657 --> 00:15:25.157
Let's see.

00:15:25.157 --> 00:15:27.760
There's steel, and
there's cement.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:28.427
Look at that.

00:15:28.427 --> 00:15:30.162
There's the world
population right there,

00:15:30.162 --> 00:15:36.969
and this runs out in 2009,
2010, '11, but it's continuing.

00:15:36.969 --> 00:15:42.841
It's continuing because it's
not just that the population is

00:15:42.841 --> 00:15:45.744
growing, it's that the
places that the population is

00:15:45.744 --> 00:15:49.682
growing the most are
also industrializing.

00:15:49.682 --> 00:15:51.917
And that means building.

00:15:51.917 --> 00:15:54.320
You go to some of these places
and there's a new building.

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:59.658
Literally every block, there's
a new building being built.

00:15:59.658 --> 00:16:02.194
That takes this stuff.

00:16:02.194 --> 00:16:05.264
That takes a lot
of ovens at a lot

00:16:05.264 --> 00:16:08.534
of really high temperatures.

00:16:08.534 --> 00:16:15.341
Just the cement
alone is accounting

00:16:15.341 --> 00:16:22.681
for 7% of all CO2 emissions,
all CO2 emissions on the planet,

00:16:22.681 --> 00:16:28.220
7% of that goes into simply
making this magic liquid stone,

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:29.455
this powder.

00:16:29.455 --> 00:16:32.091
And at the core of it,
what is at the core of it?

00:16:32.091 --> 00:16:34.326
It's chemistry.

00:16:34.326 --> 00:16:35.127
It's clinker.

00:16:35.127 --> 00:16:36.161
It's called the clinker.

00:16:36.161 --> 00:16:36.895
What is a clinker?

00:16:36.895 --> 00:16:37.930
It's this thing.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:41.467
It's simply a calcium
silicate group.

00:16:41.467 --> 00:16:44.003
Making that is what
takes all this energy.

00:16:44.003 --> 00:16:48.807
It takes half of it at
least, making that right.

00:16:48.807 --> 00:16:52.411
And as always, the
chemistry gives us

00:16:52.411 --> 00:16:58.250
the chance to get out of our
constrained optimization.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:01.453
And so you've got to make this
mixture of different types

00:17:01.453 --> 00:17:02.221
of synthetic rock.

00:17:02.221 --> 00:17:04.289
You've got to make mixtures
of alite and belite.

00:17:04.289 --> 00:17:05.357
They have different names.

00:17:07.693 --> 00:17:11.296
And you might be able to
make more belite, which

00:17:11.296 --> 00:17:12.263
takes less energy.

00:17:12.263 --> 00:17:14.633
You can cool your
oven down by 300 C

00:17:14.633 --> 00:17:18.069
to make belite
compared to alite.

00:17:18.069 --> 00:17:20.571
But then even though
it's structurally OK,

00:17:20.571 --> 00:17:23.409
it takes 90 days to dry.

00:17:23.409 --> 00:17:24.276
So you can't do that.

00:17:24.276 --> 00:17:27.813
So you've got to spend the
300 extra Celsius to make

00:17:27.813 --> 00:17:28.947
more alite.

00:17:28.947 --> 00:17:33.052
Why It's in the chemistry.

00:17:33.052 --> 00:17:34.620
It's in the chemistry.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:38.023
And understanding,
what seems like

00:17:38.023 --> 00:17:40.959
not a very interesting-- cement,
is it really that interesting?

00:17:40.959 --> 00:17:43.062
You bet it is.

00:17:43.062 --> 00:17:49.168
7% of all CO2 emissions is
just making this one material,

00:17:49.168 --> 00:17:51.470
and we're making a lot of it.

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:52.538
We're making a lot of it.

00:17:52.538 --> 00:17:55.240
There's a 164
tower in Dubai that

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:59.711
opened in 2010 that
took a billion kilograms

00:17:59.711 --> 00:18:00.345
of this stuff.

00:18:03.715 --> 00:18:06.185
Now, this makes a lot of CO2,
and I've talked about CO2

00:18:06.185 --> 00:18:07.820
throughout the semester.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:10.222
And so one thing I
wanted to leave you with

00:18:10.222 --> 00:18:12.224
was also some things that
we're doing about it.

00:18:12.224 --> 00:18:15.027
What do we do about CO2?

00:18:15.027 --> 00:18:19.131
So how is carbon capture
and sequestration going?

00:18:19.131 --> 00:18:21.100
Can we take it all out?

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:23.869
We put it in, can
we take it out?

00:18:23.869 --> 00:18:26.171
And so I'll share
a couple of things.

00:18:26.171 --> 00:18:30.809
So one of the problems
with CO2 capture

00:18:30.809 --> 00:18:35.013
is that if you don't do it
right where you make it,

00:18:35.013 --> 00:18:36.982
then you have to transport
it, and it turns out

00:18:36.982 --> 00:18:39.952
that's actually really
hard to transport CO2.

00:18:39.952 --> 00:18:41.386
And there's a lot
of problems that

00:18:41.386 --> 00:18:42.588
have happened over the years.

00:18:42.588 --> 00:18:44.189
In the '80s, there
was a leak that

00:18:44.189 --> 00:18:46.291
killed thousands of people.

00:18:46.291 --> 00:18:49.394
There have been
massive leaks in Canada

00:18:49.394 --> 00:18:51.630
in transporting this material.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:55.100
So transporting is
actually really hard to do.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:56.335
There's metal pipe corrosion.

00:18:56.335 --> 00:19:00.506
This is a great chemistry
problem unsolved.

00:19:00.506 --> 00:19:03.909
And then the next part
of CO2 sequestration

00:19:03.909 --> 00:19:05.944
is one is transporting
it that's challenging,

00:19:05.944 --> 00:19:07.146
and the other is storing it.

00:19:07.146 --> 00:19:08.514
What do you do with it?

00:19:08.514 --> 00:19:11.049
Let's suppose we
could capture it.

00:19:11.049 --> 00:19:12.217
Well, then what?

00:19:12.217 --> 00:19:14.853
And there's ideas around this.

00:19:14.853 --> 00:19:18.891
So you could pump it maybe
underground, geosequestration.

00:19:18.891 --> 00:19:21.627
That's not a sure thing.

00:19:21.627 --> 00:19:23.795
We don't know, we
don't understand

00:19:23.795 --> 00:19:26.965
at those scales exactly how well
we can in case this material

00:19:26.965 --> 00:19:29.735
and how long it lasts, so a
lot of questions with that.

00:19:29.735 --> 00:19:33.138
There's a whole line of thinking
on putting it in the ocean.

00:19:36.008 --> 00:19:36.775
Right?

00:19:36.775 --> 00:19:38.744
Really?

00:19:38.744 --> 00:19:43.549
So you want to fix the
CO2 problem by acidifying

00:19:43.549 --> 00:19:45.884
the oceans even faster?

00:19:45.884 --> 00:19:47.019
OK.

00:19:47.019 --> 00:19:50.022
Interesting.

00:19:50.022 --> 00:19:53.358
So transport and sequestration
is a big problem,

00:19:53.358 --> 00:19:56.595
but what about technologies
where you simply sequester it

00:19:56.595 --> 00:20:00.933
right there where you
make it, like clean coal.

00:20:00.933 --> 00:20:03.835
You may have heard of
clean coal, so clean coal.

00:20:03.835 --> 00:20:06.004
So I'm going to look at a
case study of a clean coal

00:20:06.004 --> 00:20:07.573
project in the US.

00:20:07.573 --> 00:20:11.143
In 2005, I got really excited
because eight companies joined,

00:20:11.143 --> 00:20:13.679
and it was called Future Gen,
and the US government was going

00:20:13.679 --> 00:20:15.280
to put a lot of money into it.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.449
They were going to
capture 90% of the CO2.

00:20:17.449 --> 00:20:19.451
You get a little
bit less efficiency.

00:20:19.451 --> 00:20:20.953
Depending on how
you think about it,

00:20:20.953 --> 00:20:23.522
it could be 30% less
efficiency, I mean, less power

00:20:23.522 --> 00:20:26.892
out of the plant, but
you sequester the CO2.

00:20:26.892 --> 00:20:30.095
And in 2009, construction
was planned in Illinois.

00:20:30.095 --> 00:20:31.597
In 2010, it was canceled.

00:20:31.597 --> 00:20:33.832
In 2010, it was restarted.

00:20:33.832 --> 00:20:39.004
In 2012, there was all sorts
of discussions with the DOE

00:20:39.004 --> 00:20:42.107
that agreed to pay $1.1 billion.

00:20:42.107 --> 00:20:44.643
It was all located, and then
the companies dropped out

00:20:44.643 --> 00:20:45.911
and the DOE canceled projects.

00:20:45.911 --> 00:20:47.312
So that was 10 years.

00:20:47.312 --> 00:20:51.250
10 years and $200 million of
taxpayer dollars and absolutely

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:53.418
nothing has come out of it.

00:20:53.418 --> 00:20:58.523
So one of the things I want to
point out about this direction,

00:20:58.523 --> 00:21:00.792
I'm not saying that it's
not a good direction

00:21:00.792 --> 00:21:03.161
to keep pursuing.

00:21:03.161 --> 00:21:05.864
But the timescales are long.

00:21:05.864 --> 00:21:08.233
So we need to be able
to make decisions that

00:21:08.233 --> 00:21:10.135
can cover longer timescales.

00:21:10.135 --> 00:21:12.638
That's a huge part of
the challenge with this.

00:21:12.638 --> 00:21:15.274
It's a policy challenge as
much as it is a technical one.

00:21:18.277 --> 00:21:22.748
And tying this
back to the cement,

00:21:22.748 --> 00:21:25.951
there is some really
interesting research

00:21:25.951 --> 00:21:28.654
that's going on in
actually instead of--

00:21:28.654 --> 00:21:29.888
OK, so you've got the CO2.

00:21:29.888 --> 00:21:31.857
It comes out when you
make it because the ovens

00:21:31.857 --> 00:21:34.826
and you're burning stuff, but
what if you could take that CO2

00:21:34.826 --> 00:21:37.796
and just put it into
the thing you're making.

00:21:37.796 --> 00:21:39.931
And by the way, if
what you're making

00:21:39.931 --> 00:21:43.268
is a huge scale like
cement, then maybe you

00:21:43.268 --> 00:21:45.370
can put a lot of it
in, and maybe that

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:47.105
could have a real global impact.

00:21:47.105 --> 00:21:52.044
So there's a lot of interest
in substantial global carbon

00:21:52.044 --> 00:21:54.079
uptake by cement carbonation.

00:21:54.079 --> 00:21:56.081
So maybe you could get
some of it in the cement.

00:21:56.081 --> 00:21:57.249
I like this paper.

00:21:57.249 --> 00:21:59.318
I'm pointing it out
because they say that they

00:21:59.318 --> 00:22:01.753
used Fick's diffusion law.

00:22:01.753 --> 00:22:04.323
They modeled the carbon uptake
by applying Fick's diffusion

00:22:04.323 --> 00:22:04.823
law.

00:22:04.823 --> 00:22:06.058
Yeah.

00:22:06.058 --> 00:22:06.892
It actually matters.

00:22:09.428 --> 00:22:11.463
Right?

00:22:11.463 --> 00:22:13.465
You can do this too now.

00:22:13.465 --> 00:22:16.935
And just a few weeks ago
on CNN, this concrete, yes,

00:22:16.935 --> 00:22:18.904
concrete is going high tech.

00:22:18.904 --> 00:22:19.571
Why?

00:22:19.571 --> 00:22:22.507
Because this company has
managed to figure out

00:22:22.507 --> 00:22:25.410
how to put a bunch of CO2
into the concrete while

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:28.213
they make it, and it doesn't
change the mechanical integrity

00:22:28.213 --> 00:22:29.548
of the concrete.

00:22:29.548 --> 00:22:30.649
And it's in there.

00:22:30.649 --> 00:22:32.651
It's actually in there.

00:22:32.651 --> 00:22:34.486
This is all chemistry.

00:22:34.486 --> 00:22:35.387
This is all chemistry.

00:22:35.387 --> 00:22:38.690
So this is, I think, another
very interesting direction

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:41.993
where technology can
play a vital role

00:22:41.993 --> 00:22:44.896
in this particular
global challenge.

