But anyway, I'm thankful
to all of these resources

00:28:38.116 --> 00:28:39.184
that we have used.

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:49.828
And so I'm going to give
you some closing comments.

00:28:49.828 --> 00:28:54.499
I started this semester
off in the first lecture,

00:28:54.499 --> 00:28:56.568
and I asked you all a question.

00:28:56.568 --> 00:28:59.170
And the question was,
why are you here?

00:28:59.170 --> 00:29:03.541
And I said really, really,
really, why are you here.

00:29:03.541 --> 00:29:05.343
Why did you come to MIT?

00:29:05.343 --> 00:29:08.780
And I asked you,
did you come here

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:12.550
so you can just phone it in,
get a stamp in four years,

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:14.452
and go out?

00:29:14.452 --> 00:29:16.821
Did you come here so
you can walk around

00:29:16.821 --> 00:29:20.091
some place in privilege and
look down on other people?

00:29:20.091 --> 00:29:22.260
That is not why you're here.

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:25.296
That is not why you came to MIT.

00:29:25.296 --> 00:29:29.300
You came to MIT because
you know that this

00:29:29.300 --> 00:29:32.937
is where we will make
the transition from being

00:29:32.937 --> 00:29:36.207
able to answer any question to
knowing which question to ask.

00:29:36.207 --> 00:29:38.810
That's the transition
from student to scholar.

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:44.215
And you know that you came here
because you have a passion,

00:29:44.215 --> 00:29:47.252
and you want to come to a place
where you can take that passion

00:29:47.252 --> 00:29:50.255
and you can try to solve
really hard problems together

00:29:50.255 --> 00:29:51.623
to make the world
a better place.

00:29:51.623 --> 00:29:53.324
Those are reasons
why you came here.

00:29:53.324 --> 00:29:58.163
You came here because you
know that progress does not

00:29:58.163 --> 00:30:00.765
happen because of success.

00:30:00.765 --> 00:30:01.699
It doesn't.

00:30:01.699 --> 00:30:03.334
It happens entirely
because of what

00:30:03.334 --> 00:30:05.603
you choose to do with failure.

00:30:05.603 --> 00:30:10.942
That's MIT, that's the MIT way,
and that's why you're here,

00:30:10.942 --> 00:30:15.146
and I asked you to think about
that on the first lecture.

00:30:15.146 --> 00:30:18.249
And then I gave you a
whole lot of examples

00:30:18.249 --> 00:30:19.117
throughout the class.

00:30:19.117 --> 00:30:21.186
I tried to connect our
learning and solid state

00:30:21.186 --> 00:30:24.889
chemistry to global challenges,
to global challenges

00:30:24.889 --> 00:30:27.192
that we face.

00:30:27.192 --> 00:30:29.260
And I tried to do that.

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:30.962
So I had 36 opportunities.

00:30:30.962 --> 00:30:33.031
There's many more, so I
had to pick and choose

00:30:33.031 --> 00:30:36.801
carefully to try to give you a
sense of some of these things.

00:30:39.337 --> 00:30:42.106
And the thing is about
these challenges, one I just

00:30:42.106 --> 00:30:47.145
talked about in many others,
it's not that these are things

00:30:47.145 --> 00:30:51.249
that we need to do soon.

00:30:51.249 --> 00:30:55.253
There is an urgency of
now about these things.

00:30:55.253 --> 00:30:56.955
There is an urgency of now.

00:30:56.955 --> 00:31:01.926
There's no more time
to talk about it.

00:31:01.926 --> 00:31:05.930
And so these challenges, it's
not like something happens over

00:31:05.930 --> 00:31:09.634
here, happens over there.

00:31:09.634 --> 00:31:11.769
These are planetary challenges.

00:31:11.769 --> 00:31:13.438
These cover the whole thing.

00:31:13.438 --> 00:31:18.276
So it's all on the table.

00:31:18.276 --> 00:31:21.279
It's all on the line.

00:31:21.279 --> 00:31:23.214
And by the way, if it's
a planetary challenge,

00:31:23.214 --> 00:31:25.250
it means the
challenge doesn't care

00:31:25.250 --> 00:31:27.952
if you're in a blue
State or a red State

00:31:27.952 --> 00:31:31.055
or if you live in Europe
or Africa or here.

00:31:31.055 --> 00:31:32.790
It doesn't care.

00:31:32.790 --> 00:31:35.927
So we better figure out how to
get our act together and work

00:31:35.927 --> 00:31:39.063
together to solve these
very hard problems.

00:31:39.063 --> 00:31:41.266
And that's why you're here.

00:31:41.266 --> 00:31:43.635
That's why you're here.

00:31:43.635 --> 00:31:45.503
And so on the first
lecture, I asked

00:31:45.503 --> 00:31:46.938
you to think about
why you're here,

00:31:46.938 --> 00:31:49.374
and on this very
last lecture, I'm

00:31:49.374 --> 00:31:53.411
asking you to think about what
you want to do about that.

00:31:53.411 --> 00:31:56.381
What do you want
to do about that?

00:31:56.381 --> 00:31:59.684
And there's a lot of
things you can do.

00:31:59.684 --> 00:32:03.922
I don't mean that you have to
work on one of these things

00:32:03.922 --> 00:32:05.290
we've talked about
in this class.

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:06.257
That's not what I mean.

00:32:06.257 --> 00:32:08.760
I mean, think about what it
is that you really want to do

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:11.663
and how you want to do
it, and then go for it.

00:32:14.265 --> 00:32:16.968
You might have figured that
out before you came here.

00:32:16.968 --> 00:32:19.604
You may know-- gesundheit--

00:32:19.604 --> 00:32:21.306
already way before you got here.

00:32:21.306 --> 00:32:24.075
You may be figuring
it out right now.

00:32:24.075 --> 00:32:29.047
You may have no clue what you
want to do when you graduate.

00:32:29.047 --> 00:32:31.382
All of those are fine.

00:32:31.382 --> 00:32:33.618
All of those are fine.

00:32:33.618 --> 00:32:36.020
But what I want
to leave you with

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:39.891
is a framework and some
thinking that what can I

00:32:39.891 --> 00:32:42.860
leave you with as a
suggestion, as a framework.

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:45.663
And it really comes down
to three ingredients,

00:32:45.663 --> 00:32:47.165
three ingredients
that I'm asking

00:32:47.165 --> 00:32:49.400
you to think about
when you go off

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:52.704
and do, when you leave here,
when you go off and do.

00:32:52.704 --> 00:32:53.738
What are you going to do?

00:32:53.738 --> 00:32:55.239
How are you going to frame this?

00:32:55.239 --> 00:32:59.444
And so the first
ingredient is kindness.

00:32:59.444 --> 00:33:00.611
Now OK.

00:33:00.611 --> 00:33:01.779
Bear with me.

00:33:01.779 --> 00:33:04.882
Because you're all thinking,
what is he talking about?

00:33:04.882 --> 00:33:08.252
This is a chemistry class, and
you're talking about kindness.

00:33:12.323 --> 00:33:15.259
But you see, the
thing is, I'm trying

00:33:15.259 --> 00:33:18.730
to think about what is a
framework that if you follow it

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:20.965
you will do good.

00:33:20.965 --> 00:33:24.002
You will help change this
world for the better, which

00:33:24.002 --> 00:33:25.636
is what we do at MIT.

00:33:25.636 --> 00:33:27.338
It's how we think.

00:33:27.338 --> 00:33:29.540
And the thing is I don't
care if you define kindness

00:33:29.540 --> 00:33:36.614
as love or as helping
someone or as community or as

00:33:36.614 --> 00:33:39.183
on one of those dates when
the candle was in the room

00:33:39.183 --> 00:33:42.086
and you did the calculation
and you saved the day

00:33:42.086 --> 00:33:43.955
because you knew oxygen
was going to run out.

00:33:43.955 --> 00:33:45.423
That's all kindness.

00:33:45.423 --> 00:33:46.424
All of that is kindness.

00:33:46.424 --> 00:33:49.894
But the thing is that if you
have kindness in what you do,

00:33:49.894 --> 00:33:51.362
it's really hard to do bad.

00:33:54.532 --> 00:33:56.567
Is that under the hood?

00:33:56.567 --> 00:33:58.069
Is there an element of kindness?

00:33:58.069 --> 00:33:59.937
You can't have too much of it.

00:33:59.937 --> 00:34:02.340
We need that vector.

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:05.543
We need that vector
in this world.

00:34:05.543 --> 00:34:07.678
So that's one ingredient,
and the second ingredient

00:34:07.678 --> 00:34:08.212
is knowledge.

00:34:08.212 --> 00:34:11.315
Now, what I mean by this is
acquisition of knowledge.

00:34:11.315 --> 00:34:13.384
And we have done a lot of
that here in this class,

00:34:13.384 --> 00:34:15.152
and you do a lot of
that at MIT, and we

00:34:15.152 --> 00:34:18.156
celebrate these acquisitions.

00:34:18.156 --> 00:34:20.291
What I'm asking you to
do is think about this

00:34:20.291 --> 00:34:21.592
as you go forward.

00:34:21.592 --> 00:34:23.828
How are you acquiring knowledge?

00:34:23.828 --> 00:34:25.530
How are you learning?

00:34:25.530 --> 00:34:26.397
That sounds cliche.

00:34:26.397 --> 00:34:28.232
Always learn.

00:34:28.232 --> 00:34:30.034
You guys have to
keep doing that.

00:34:30.034 --> 00:34:32.136
You have to keep doing
that because, for you, it's

00:34:32.136 --> 00:34:36.274
nourishment, and it's
another one of these vectors.

00:34:36.274 --> 00:34:38.810
You can't do any
harm by learning.

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:42.580
You can only do good for
yourself and for everyone

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:44.315
that you care about.

00:34:44.315 --> 00:34:47.351
So keep doing it.

00:34:47.351 --> 00:34:49.387
The rate might change
of learning acquisition

00:34:49.387 --> 00:34:52.690
as a reaction, the rate
constant might be lower.

00:34:52.690 --> 00:34:56.327
We know places where
that's already the case.

00:34:56.327 --> 00:35:00.898
And that's OK.

00:35:00.898 --> 00:35:04.502
Maybe the order is
lower too down there,

00:35:04.502 --> 00:35:07.605
but I think they probably
are learning something.

00:35:10.508 --> 00:35:12.710
Keep that going.

00:35:12.710 --> 00:35:14.078
And the last thing is passion.

00:35:14.078 --> 00:35:17.915
And this involves your talents.

00:35:17.915 --> 00:35:20.585
You all have talents already.

00:35:20.585 --> 00:35:23.521
You all have them because
that's part of who you are

00:35:23.521 --> 00:35:25.957
and why you're here.

00:35:25.957 --> 00:35:30.061
Those are things that
you bring a passion to.

00:35:30.061 --> 00:35:32.096
What are those talents?

00:35:32.096 --> 00:35:34.866
Maybe some of them are
hidden, but work on them

00:35:34.866 --> 00:35:35.933
and pursue them.

00:35:35.933 --> 00:35:37.835
Do things that you
literally are so

00:35:37.835 --> 00:35:39.570
excited about you
can't sleep sometimes

00:35:39.570 --> 00:35:41.706
because of the excitement.

00:35:41.706 --> 00:35:43.674
That's what I'm talking about.

00:35:43.674 --> 00:35:45.343
Follow that.

00:35:45.343 --> 00:35:48.179
Feed it.

00:35:48.179 --> 00:35:54.051
And while you do, lose the fear.

00:35:54.051 --> 00:35:57.121
Avoid greed, and
embrace challenge.

00:35:57.121 --> 00:35:59.557
That's what MT is.

00:35:59.557 --> 00:36:01.792
That's why we're here.

00:36:01.792 --> 00:36:04.428
That's why we're here.

00:36:04.428 --> 00:36:06.063
So I feel like these
three ingredients,

00:36:06.063 --> 00:36:08.399
I've thought about this, and
I thought about these three

00:36:08.399 --> 00:36:10.001
ingredients, and
I've thought about--

00:36:10.001 --> 00:36:11.869
we have these already.

00:36:11.869 --> 00:36:17.108
You all have these ingredients
because that is also MIT,

00:36:17.108 --> 00:36:20.344
and you are here,
and you are MIT.

00:36:20.344 --> 00:36:23.915
And so what I'm asking you
to do is to hold onto them

00:36:23.915 --> 00:36:25.149
and keep thinking about them.

00:36:27.652 --> 00:36:32.056
There's a lot of reasons
why I love to teach,

00:36:32.056 --> 00:36:35.927
but one of the big ones is when
I think about the challenges

00:36:35.927 --> 00:36:38.829
that I share with
you guys and I have

00:36:38.829 --> 00:36:41.365
36 chances to look
up in the room

00:36:41.365 --> 00:36:42.767
and I think about
those challenges

00:36:42.767 --> 00:36:44.235
and I think about
these ingredients

00:36:44.235 --> 00:36:46.904
and I think about all
of you, I actually

00:36:46.904 --> 00:36:49.106
feel like we have a shot.

00:36:49.106 --> 00:36:50.708
I actually feel that.

00:36:50.708 --> 00:36:56.414
And so with that, good
luck on the final,

00:36:56.414 --> 00:36:57.615
and have a fantastic break.

00:36:59.717 --> 00:37:01.219
Thank you so much.

00:37:01.219 --> 00:37:02.220
Thank you.

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:03.688
Thank you, guys.

00:37:03.688 --> 00:37:05.156
Thank you, guys.

00:37:05.156 --> 00:37:06.157
Thank you.

00:37:06.157 --> 00:37:07.158
Thank you.

00:37:07.158 --> 00:37:09.126
Thank you so much.

00:37:09.126 --> 00:37:10.094
Thank you.

00:37:13.431 --> 00:37:14.332
You guys are amazing.

00:37:17.501 --> 00:37:18.502
Thank you.

00:37:18.502 --> 00:37:19.470
Thank you.

00:37:26.844 --> 00:37:29.280
Thank you all so much.

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:30.915
I'm going to have to
leave here, or I'm

00:37:30.915 --> 00:37:33.417
going to get really emotional.

00:37:33.417 --> 00:37:35.253
I've clearly got a lot
of other dance moves

00:37:35.253 --> 00:37:39.890
the TAs have helped me,
so I can practice those.

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:41.425
Thank you so much.

00:37:41.425 --> 00:37:42.994
That's just incredible.

00:37:42.994 --> 00:37:45.596
You guys are all incredible,
and this has been incredible.

00:37:45.596 --> 00:37:50.034
So we do have 10 minutes.

00:37:50.034 --> 00:37:50.901
I'm going to leave.

00:37:50.901 --> 00:37:53.204
So you guys can
fill evaluations out

00:37:53.204 --> 00:37:55.940
without me being in the room.

00:37:55.940 --> 00:37:58.576
And so you can say
whatever you want.

00:37:58.576 --> 00:38:01.612
Again, don't let the free
T-shirts being thrown at you

00:38:01.612 --> 00:38:06.617
influence your opinion
or the free candy.

00:38:06.617 --> 00:38:12.623
But thanks, everyone,
for a fantastic semester.

00:38:12.623 --> 00:38:14.325
Thank you.

00:38:14.325 --> 00:38:14.825
Thanks.

00:38:17.762 --> 00:38:19.096
That was amazing, you guys.

